{
 "sheet": 1,
 "name": "SN74LS07N",
 "kind": "logic",
 "manufacturer": "Texas Instruments",
 "title": "SN74LS07 hex buffer/driver, open-collector outputs rated 30 V, 40 mA sink",
 "source": {
  "document": "SDLS021D",
  "revision": "May 1990, revised April 2016",
  "pages": 25
 },
 "summary": "Six non-inverting LS-TTL buffers, each with an open-collector output that withstands 30 V when off and sinks up to 40 mA when on. A high input leaves the output off (high impedance); a low input pulls it low. It runs from 5 V (4.75 V to 5.25 V), takes TTL input levels with clamp diodes on the inputs, and is used to raise TTL levels to MOS levels, to drive lamps, LEDs and relays, and to build wired-AND nodes. Typical dissipation is 140 mW and the average propagation delay is 12 ns. Offered in 14-pin SOIC (D), SSOP (DB), PDIP (N) and SO (NS) packages, 0 °C to 70 °C.",
 "key_figures": [
