 "equations": [],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Function",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Each of the six channels is a non-inverting buffer whose output transistor is an open collector. With the input high the transistor is off and the pin floats (Hi-Z); with the input low it conducts and pulls the pin low. An external pull-up sets the high level."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Wired connection",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Open-collector outputs from this or other devices may be joined on one node, giving an active-high wired-AND or an active-low wired-OR."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Level translation",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Inputs take up to 5.25 V and can be driven by 5 V CMOS, 3.3 V LVTTL or 2.5 V logic; outputs may be pulled up to 30 V. The part therefore shifts TTL levels up to MOS levels or to a higher supply in a mixed-voltage system."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output drive",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "An output sinks up to 40 mA, enough for indicator lamps, LEDs, relays or several inputs. It is also specified for driving TTL inputs and works with most TTL families. Keep each output's load current at or below 40 mA and never pull an output above 30 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Inputs",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Each input has a clamp diode to ground that reduces transmission-line effects (VIK –1.5 V max at –12 mA). An input must sit at a valid level, at least VIH (2 V) or at most VIL (0.8 V); an input in between leaves the output in an unknown state."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Gate circuit",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Each gate is a bipolar circuit ending in an open-collector output transistor. Its nominal resistors are 9 kΩ at the input, 5 kΩ, 1 kΩ and two of 2 kΩ."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Power supply",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "VCC may be anywhere in the 4.75 V to 5.25 V recommended range. A single-supply device such as this one takes a 0.1 µF bypass capacitor at its VCC pin, placed as close to the pin as possible; 0.1 µF and 1 µF in parallel are a common pair for rejecting noise at different frequencies."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Unused inputs and outputs",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "No input may float: an open input sits at an undefined voltage and the channel's state is undefined. Tie each unused input to VCC or GND, whichever suits the layout. An unused output may be left open."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Handling",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "ESD protection is limited; store and handle the parts in conductive foam or with the leads shorted together."
  }
 ],
